    "faq_shrimp": {
      "type": "faq",
      "blocks": {
        "q_done": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How do I know when the shrimp are done?",
            "text": "<p>By shape. A raw shrimp lies straight; a cooked one curls into a loose C; an overcooked one closes into a tight O. Pull the pan off the heat at the loose C &mdash; opaque through, still plump, with a clear gap between head and tail. The sauce is thick and holds heat, so they keep tightening after the burner is off.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_bloom": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Can I just stir the spices into the sauce?",
            "text": "<p>You can, and it will taste noticeably flatter. Ground spices are fat-soluble &mdash; giving them 45 seconds in melted butter opens them up in a way stirring them into liquid does not. It is the single cheapest step in the recipe and the one that makes it taste like it took longer than it did.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_blender": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Is blending the hot pumpkin safe?",
            "text": "<p>With one precaution. Hot food turns to steam in a sealed blender jug and can force the lid off. Let the pumpkin cool for a few minutes first, fill the jug no more than two-thirds, remove the centre cap so steam escapes, hold a folded towel over the opening and start on the lowest speed before working up.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_squash": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What if I can't find kabocha?",
            "text": "<p>Butternut is the easiest swap and slightly sweeter; sugar pumpkin works too. Whatever you use, roast rather than boil it &mdash; the caramelised edges are where the sauce gets its depth, and boiled squash gives you a thin, vegetal sauce no amount of spice will rescue.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_heat": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How hot is this, and can I dial it down?",
            "text": "<p>Mild to medium as written. One Fresno split between the sauce and the garnish gives warmth rather than burn. For less, seed the chile or use only the half that goes in early &mdash; the raw slices scattered at the end are the sharper hit. For more, add a second, or a pinch of cayenne with the garam masala.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        },
        "q_thaw": {
          "type": "question",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How should I thaw Seatopia seafood?",
            "text": "<p>Overnight in the refrigerator in the sealed pack, about 12 hours, then pat the shrimp dry. In a hurry, submerge the sealed pack in a bowl of chilled water for 20 to 30 minutes, changing the water once. Never at room temperature, and never in warm water.</p>",
            "highlighted_text": "none",
            "highlighted_scribble": "circle"
          }
        }
      },
      "block_order": [
        "q_done",
        "q_bloom",
        "q_blender",
        "q_squash",
        "q_heat",
        "q_thaw"
      ],
